-- ============================================================================
-- mendixlabs/mxcli#1025 — a syntax topic must resolve however it is spelled
-- ============================================================================
--
-- `mxcli syntax` advertises, in its own help:
--
--     mxcli syntax workflow user-task targeting     # Drill down to targeting
--
-- Handed over as ONE string — a quoted copy-paste, a tool wrapper, `sh -c` —
-- that returned `Unknown topic: workflow user-task targeting`, spaces and all,
-- on the reported v0.20.0 and on main. The CLI joined its arguments on "." and
-- never split them, while the REPL's HELP had resolved multi-word topics since
-- it was written: one question, two answers.
--
-- Both surfaces now share syntax.Lookup, and the grammar takes the spellings
-- the registry PRINTS: `mxcli syntax workflow` lists `workflow.user-task`, and
-- copying that into HELP used to be a parse error — a hyphen is its own token
-- (HYPHENATED_ID) and a dot is DOT, and `helpStatement` named neither.
--
-- The CLI half is not MDL; it is pinned by cmd/mxcli/cmd_syntax_test.go and
-- cmd/mxcli/syntax/topic_test.go, including a guard that every registered path
-- is reachable by all three spellings.
--
-- No project needed: HELP reads the embedded syntax registry.
-- ============================================================================

-- Plain words — the spelling HELP has always taken.
help workflow user task targeting;

-- Hyphenated, as the listing prints each segment.
help workflow user-task targeting;

-- The dotted path, exactly as `mxcli syntax workflow` prints it.
help workflow.user-task.targeting;

-- A hyphen in the FIRST segment, and a dot after it.
help domain-model.entity;

-- The other two topics the report names.
help workflow user-task;
help workflow parallel-split;

-- The second advertised example.
help security entity-access;
